# Week 1 Live Examples — Fundamentals of AI & ML

Two short, instructor-facing demos that turn the Week 1 slide claims into
running code. Both need **only Python 3 + NumPy** (matplotlib optional) and run
on a bare laptop or a cluster login node.

| Script | Slide segment | Proves |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `train_from_scratch.py` | S5 — Neural Networks | a neural net is *just* GEMMs + a nonlinearity + a loss + SGD — hand-coded, no PyTorch |
| `training_cost_calculator.py` | S6 — Training as a Workload | why one machine is not enough: `6·P·T` FLOPs and `16·P` bytes for real model sizes |

## Run them live

```bash
cd examples/week1_ml_foundations

# 1. Train a 2-layer MLP on two-moons; watch the loss fall in the terminal
python3 train_from_scratch.py

# 2. Reproduce the overfitting curve from Segment 2 (train falls, val turns up)
python3 train_from_scratch.py --overfit

# 3. Quantify the compute/memory walls for BERT, GPT-2, LLaMA, GPT-3
python3 training_cost_calculator.py
python3 training_cost_calculator.py --params 7e9 --tokens 2e12   # try your own
```

## Teaching notes

- **`train_from_scratch.py`** — every line maps to a slide. The `forward()` is
  two GEMMs and a ReLU; `backward()` is the chain rule written out; `step()` is
  `W -= lr * grad`. Run the clean version first (accuracy climbs to ~0.95),
  then `--overfit` to see training loss keep dropping while validation loss
  turns back up — the overfitting curve, live. If matplotlib is installed it
  saves `loss_curve.png`; otherwise it draws an ASCII curve so the picture
  always appears.
- **`training_cost_calculator.py`** — read across a large row (e.g. LLaMA-7B):
  memory already exceeds one GPU (the *memory wall*), single-GPU time is years
  (the *compute wall*), and the thousand-GPU column only helps *if* those GPUs
  cooperate near-linearly — the motivation for Weeks 2–4 and Modules 2–3.

## The discipline these set up

From Week 2 onward every demo follows the same loop the whole course repeats:
**measure → classify → change one thing → re-measure.** Each script here already
produces a *number* (accuracy, loss, FLOPs, wall-clock) rather than a vibe.
